 "script": "Good morning, Yamuna, and happy Tuesday. I hope the coffee is hot. I've gathered a few things that sit alongside the news today, and I'll begin with a book that I think you might fall for.\n\nThe Guardian has been reviewing The Song of Stork and Dromedary by the Dutch writer Anjet Daanje, and the critic's advice is simply: don't miss it. It's an epic of seven hundred and twenty pages, inspired by Emily Brontë, and it follows a Yorkshire girl's afterlife across three centuries. A ghostly heroine, a vast sweep of time and a Brontë shadow over the whole thing. It sounds like a book to disappear into on a long winter weekend.\n\nStaying with fiction, but in a quieter key, Radio 4's Open Book this week has Mary Lawson talking about her new novel, Solace in Winter. It takes her back to the Canadian town of Solace, and she also talks about the remote family cabin that stands behind the story. It's about twenty minutes, so it's ideal for the drive, and a pleasure if you like a novelist describing the place a book grew from.\n\nNow to paintings, which I know you like to see in conversation with words. The Guardian's review of Nan Goldin's show at the Hayward Gallery is quite something. The critic calls her the greatest American artist since Warhol, and traces echoes of Caravaggio, of Greek myth and of Stendhal Syndrome through her photographs and her film. Stendhal Syndrome, that dizzying overwhelm in front of great beauty, is named after a writer, so it feels like a theme for you. The review is worth a few minutes, even if you can't get to London.\n\nAnd while we're on the meeting of the arts, there's a good episode of Start the Week on Radio 4 from Monday. It begins with Jonathan Coe's novel Lessons in Harmony, which imagines a friendship between Ravel and Vaughan Williams. Then the guests move on to art crossing borders, and to the creative connections between music and architecture. It's a lovely way to spend a drive, with a novelist's imagination and some very good listening ideas.\n\nOne more thing for your dance-documentary side. CiberCuba reports that Rosario Suárez, known as Charín, a former prima ballerina of the National Ballet of Cuba, is presenting in Spain a documentary about her life and her exile. I don't have more detail than that yet, but a dancer telling her own story of leaving home is the sort of film worth keeping an eye out for.\n\nAnd one good thing before you go. On the BBC World Service's The Documentary, there's a programme about women in Nepal challenging period myths. It brings together activists, educators and young women who are taking on old taboos, with a girl-designed app and a podcast among their tools. It's about twenty-two minutes of people changing things from the inside, which is always good to hear.\n\nHave a lovely Tuesday, Yamuna, and I'll see you tomorrow.",
 "words": 498,
 "teaser": "A Brontë-haunted epic, Mary Lawson's Solace, Nan Goldin and a Cuban ballerina's story",
